-- title:
--   Proposition 2.1: the conditional risk measure of $\mathfrak A_i$ equals the conditional DR functional of $\mathfrak M_\nu=\sum_i\nu_i\mathfrak A_i$
-- statement:
--   Let $(\Omega,\mathcal F)$ be a measurable space and $\mathcal G$ the finite sigma subalgebra generated by a finite partition $\{\varpi_i\}_{i\in\mathcal I}$ of $\Omega$ into nonempty measurable sets. For each $i\in\mathcal I$ let $\mathfrak A_i$ be an arbitrary **nonempty** set of probability measures on $(\Omega,\mathcal F)$ supported on $\varpi_i$, and let $\mathcal R_{|\mathcal G}$ be the conditional risk measure they determine,
--
--   $$
--   \mathcal R_{|\mathcal G}(Z)(\varpi_i)=\sup_{Q\in\mathfrak A_i}\mathbb E_Q[Z]\qquad(2.7).
--   $$
--
--   Let $\nu_i>0$, $i\in\mathcal I$, be positive probabilities ($\sum_i\nu_i=1$), let $\mathfrak M_\nu=\sum_i\nu_i\mathfrak A_i=\{\sum_i\nu_iQ_i: Q_i\in\mathfrak A_i\}$ (2.9), and let $\mathcal R^\star_{|\mathcal G}$ be the conditional distributionally robust functional of $\mathfrak M=\mathfrak M_\nu$,
--
--   $$
--   \mathcal R^\star_{|\mathcal G}(Z)(\varpi_i)=\sup_{Q\in\mathfrak M_\nu,\;Q(\varpi_i)\neq0}\frac{1}{Q(\varpi_i)}\int_{\varpi_i}Z\,dQ\qquad(2.6).
--   $$
--
--   **Proposition 2.1.** For every measurable $Z:\Omega\to\mathbb R$ that is integrable with respect to every measure in every $\mathfrak A_i$,
--
--   $$
--   \mathcal R_{|\mathcal G}(Z)=\mathcal R^\star_{|\mathcal G}(Z)\qquad\text{on every atom }\varpi_i .
--   $$
--
--   So every conditional risk measure built from atom-wise ambiguity sets is a conditional distributionally robust functional, for an ambiguity set on $\Omega$ that can be chosen with any positive atom probabilities $\nu$. This is the bridge between the risk-averse and the distributionally robust constructions of conditional functionals that the paper compares.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $\Omega$ is a general measurable space; only the partition is finite. $\mathcal G$-measurable variables are represented by their atom values, functions $\mathcal I\to\overline{\mathbb R}$, and the suprema are taken in the extended reals.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: the paper says it "mainly" considers a finite underlying measurable space (p.3) → Ω is an arbitrary measurable space, only the partition is finite; reason: §2 states its definitions for a general (Ω, ℱ) with finite 𝒢; a finite Ω with ℱ = all subsets is a special case
--   - generalization: R(Z) is assumed finite valued (p.3) and the functionals map into ℝ → values in EReal, no finiteness assumption; reason: whenever the paper's finiteness assumption holds both sides are the same real numbers, so the statement specializes to the paper's
--   - reading: Z ∈ 𝒵, "an appropriate linear space of measurable variables" with E_Q[Z] well-defined for every Q ∈ 𝔐 = 𝔐_ν (p.3) → Measurable Z and Integrable Z Q for every Q in every 𝔄ᵢ; reason: since each νᵢ > 0 and each 𝔄ⱼ is nonempty, integrability for every member of 𝔐_ν is equivalent to integrability for every member of every 𝔄ᵢ
--   - encoding: "supported on the set ϖᵢ" → Q (ϖ.cell i)ᶜ = 0; reason: the support condition the paper uses for P|ϖᵢ of (2.4)
--   - encoding: a 𝒢-measurable variable constant on each atom → a function I → EReal (its value on each atom); reason: the paper's notation Z(ϖᵢ) of (2.3); the same objects
--   - encoding: νᵢ Qᵢ in (2.9) → ENNReal.ofReal (ν i) • q i; reason: Lean's measures take scalars in [0, ∞]; νᵢ > 0, so the same measures
-- source:
--   Shapiro & Pichler, Conditional Distributionally Robust Functionals, preprint (October 9, 2022), Proposition 2.1, p.5, with (2.6), p.4, and (2.7), (2.9), p.5

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_CondDRO_FinitePartition
import Definitions.Def_CondDRO_condDRF
import Definitions.Def_CondDRO_condRM
import Definitions.Def_CondDRO_mixture

open MeasureTheory

namespace CondDRO

/-- **Proposition 2.1** (Shapiro & Pichler, *Conditional Distributionally Robust Functionals*,
preprint (October 9, 2022), p.5): "For any positive probabilities νi, i ∈ ℐ, and the conditional
distributionally robust functional R⋆|𝒢 associated with the set 𝔐 = 𝔐ν of the form (2.9), we have
that R|𝒢(·) = R⋆|𝒢(·)."

Setting (pp.3–5): `(Ω, ℱ)` a measurable space; 𝒢 the finite sigma algebra generated by a finite
partition {ϖᵢ}_{i∈ℐ} into nonempty measurable atoms; for each `i` an arbitrary nonempty set 𝔄ᵢ of
probability measures supported on ϖᵢ, determining R|𝒢 by (2.7); positive probabilities νᵢ
(Σνᵢ = 1); 𝔐_ν = Σᵢ νᵢ𝔄ᵢ by (2.9); R⋆|𝒢 the functional (2.6) of 𝔐_ν. For every measurable `Z`
integrable with respect to every measure in every 𝔄ᵢ, R|𝒢(Z) = R⋆|𝒢(Z) on every atom.

**Formalization Note.** Ω is a general measurable space (§2); the only finiteness is `[Fintype I]`.
𝒢-measurable variables are their atom values, `I → EReal`, and the suprema are taken in `EReal`,
without the paper's standing assumption that R(Z) is finite. "Supported on ϖᵢ" is
`Q (ϖ.cell i)ᶜ = 0`. `νᵢ` enters (2.9) as `ENNReal.ofReal (ν i)`. -/
theorem proposition_2_1 {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] {I : Type*} [Fintype I]
    (ϖ : FinitePartition Ω I) (A : I → Set (Measure Ω))
    (hA_ne : ∀ i, (A i).Nonempty)
    (hA_prob : ∀ i, ∀ Q ∈ A i, IsProbabilityMeasure Q)
    (hA_supp : ∀ i, ∀ Q ∈ A i, Q (ϖ.cell i)ᶜ = 0)
    (ν : I → ℝ) (hν_pos : ∀ i, 0 < ν i) (hν_sum : ∑ i, ν i = 1)
    (Z : Ω → ℝ) (hZ_meas : Measurable Z) (hZ_int : ∀ i, ∀ Q ∈ A i, Integrable Z Q) :
    condRM A Z = condDRF (mixture A ν) ϖ Z := by sorry

end CondDRO
